Title

A Geometric Bifurcation Theory of Megafire Emergence: Critical Fire Number, Information Geometry, and the Controllability Threshold

Author

Ouadi Maakoul

AI

chatGpt, Kimi, Deepseek, Meta AI

Abstract

Megafires—wildfires that escape all containment efforts—are extreme events whose onset is poorly captured by empirical models. This dissertation develops a rigorous mathematical framework that identifies the dynamical threshold separating local, controllable fires from self‑sustaining megafires. We derive a dimensionless Critical Fire Number R_f from the governing reaction–diffusion–advection equations of wildland fire. A saddle‑node bifurcation of travelling combustion waves is proved to occur at a universal critical value R_f^*. Below this value, ignitions extinguish; above it, a stable propagating front emerges—the megafire state. We equip the space of environmental parameters with a Fisher–Rao metric and show that the scalar curvature diverges at the critical manifold, providing a model‑based early‑warning signal. A control‑theoretic formulation using Control Lyapunov–Barrier Functions proves that a safe, stabilising suppression policy exists if and only if R_f < R_f^*, thus giving the first mathematically precise definition of megafire uncontainability. The final part of the theory integrates these structures into a Bayesian data assimilation architecture that estimates R_f in real time and monitors the landscape’s distance to critical instability. No numerical experiments or physical data are used; the contribution is a self‑contained mathematical foundation upon which predictive systems and operational decision‑support tools can be built.

