Chapter 2

Mathematical Foundations: State Space, Stability, and Critical Transitions

In this chapter we construct the mathematical skeleton of the theory. The central objects are a dynamical system encoding wildfire physics, a dimensionless bifurcation parameter R_f, and a geometric setting that formalises risk, information flow, and control.

---

2.1 Governing Equations: A Minimal Coupled System

Wildfire propagation is a reaction–diffusion–advection (RDA) process. We adopt a canonical two‑variable model coupling temperature T(\mathbf{x},t) and fuel mass fraction Y(\mathbf{x},t) over a two‑dimensional spatial domain \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2:

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \nabla\!\cdot\!(K(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - h(T-T_\infty) + \Psi(T) Y, \\[4pt]
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\Psi(T) Y .
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.1}

Here K(T) = k + \varepsilon\sigma T^3 accounts for thermal conductivity and radiative transfer, \mathbf{w}(\mathbf{x},t) is the wind field, h a convective cooling coefficient, and T_\infty the ambient temperature. The ignition function \Psi(T) is taken as a smooth, monotonic regularisation of an Arrhenius law:

\Psi(T) = A \, S\!\left(\frac{T-\bar{T}}{\delta}\right) \exp(-T_{ac}/T),
\tag{2.2}

where S is a smooth sigmoid (e.g. logistic function), \bar{T} the nominal ignition temperature, \delta \ll 1 a sharpness parameter, and T_{ac} the activation temperature. The smoothness ensures differentiability of the forward map, a technical requirement for the data assimilation and geometric analyses. Fuel moisture effects are introduced via a slow variable m(\mathbf{x},t):

\frac{\partial m}{\partial t} = -D_m m + S_m(\mathbf{x}) - \Lambda(T) m,
\tag{2.3}

giving a slow–fast structure typical of singularly perturbed systems.

For finite‑dimensional analysis we project the field onto an aggregated state vector

\mathbf{x}(t) = (T_\text{eff}, W_\text{eff}, M_\text{eff}, V_\text{eff}, \dots) \in \mathbb{R}^n,

whose evolution is written as

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) + \boldsymbol{\xi}(t),
\tag{2.4}

with \boldsymbol{\theta} containing slowly varying climatic and topographic parameters, and \boldsymbol{\xi} a stochastic forcing term representing lightning, turbulence gusts, etc.

---

2.2 Nondimensionalisation and the Critical Fire Number R_f

Introduce reference scales: length L, velocity U, time \tau = L/U, temperature rise \Delta T_c = T_c - T_\infty with T_c a characteristic combustion temperature, and initial fuel mass fraction Y_0. Define dimensionless variables

\tilde{T} = \frac{T-T_\infty}{\Delta T_c},\quad
\tilde{Y} = \frac{Y}{Y_0},\quad
\tilde{\mathbf{x}} = \frac{\mathbf{x}}{L},\quad
\tilde{t} = \frac{t}{\tau},

and drop tildes to obtain

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \nabla\!\cdot\!(\kappa(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda\,\Psi(T) Y, \\[4pt]
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\beta\,\Psi(T) Y,
\end{aligned}
\tag{2.5}

with the dimensionless groups

\mathrm{Pe} = \frac{UL}{\alpha_0},\quad
\mathrm{St} = \frac{hL}{U},\quad
\lambda = \frac{A L \Delta H_c Y_0}{c_p \Delta T_c U},\quad
\beta = \frac{AL}{U}.

Here \Delta H_c is the combustion enthalpy, c_p the specific heat, and \alpha_0 a reference thermal diffusivity. The group \lambda measures heat release versus advective transport, while \beta governs fuel consumption.

To capture the global competition between energy production and dissipation we define the Critical Fire Number

\boxed{ R_f := \frac{\lambda}{\mathrm{St} + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}} }.
\tag{2.6}

The denominator sums the principal linear dissipative pathways: convective cooling (\mathrm{St}) and conductive/radiative diffusion (\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}). This definition is consistent with the linear stability limit in the long‑wavelength, advection‑dominated limit (see Lemma 2.1). Physically, R_f < 1 means cooling dominates; R_f > 1 means heat release overwhelms dissipation.

---

2.3 Linear Stability of the Unburnt State

For the homogeneous equilibrium T\equiv 0,\; Y\equiv 1 the linearisation of (2.5) with the smooth \Psi(T) yields \Psi'(0) = \gamma > 0 (which can be made arbitrarily small by choosing a sharp sigmoid). A normal‑mode perturbation e^{\sigma t + i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}} gives the dispersion relation

\sigma = -\Bigl(\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}|\mathbf{k}|^2 + i\mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{k} + \mathrm{St}\Bigr) + \lambda \gamma.

Lemma 2.1 (Linear instability threshold).
The unburnt equilibrium is linearly unstable if and only if R_f > 1 in the limit \mathrm{Pe}\to\infty and \gamma \to 0 such that \lambda\gamma remains finite.

Proof. The real part is \Re(\sigma) = -\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}|\mathbf{k}|^2 - \mathrm{St} + \lambda\gamma. The most dangerous mode is \mathbf{k}=0. Instability occurs for \lambda\gamma > \mathrm{St}. With the definition R_f = \lambda/(\mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}) and taking \mathrm{Pe}\to\infty we obtain the condition R_f > 1, provided \gamma is scaled appropriately. In the full nonlinear analysis, the sharp threshold \gamma\to0 is replaced by the fold bifurcation, and the critical R_f^* emerges from the travelling wave problem. ∎

Thus R_f = 1 signals the linear destabilisation of the unburnt landscape, anticipating the fully nonlinear bifurcation developed in Chapter 3.

---

2.4 Slow–Fast Structure and Critical Manifolds

With fuel moisture as the slow variable, the system is singularly perturbed: \epsilon = \tau_m/\tau_f \ll 1. In the limit \epsilon\to0 the fast (T,Y) dynamics define a critical manifold

\mathcal{C}_0 = \{ (T,Y,m) : f_{\text{fast}}(T,Y;m) = 0 \}.

Fenichel theory guarantees that normally hyperbolic portions of \mathcal{C}_0 persist as invariant manifolds \mathcal{C}_\epsilon for 0<\epsilon\ll 1. The flow on \mathcal{C}_\epsilon governs the slow evolution of fire potential. Fold points of \mathcal{C}_0 correspond to the onset of fast transitions—here, the ignition of a propagating front. This geometric singular perturbation framework underpins the rigorous bifurcation theorem.

Theorem 2.2 (Fold bifurcation of travelling waves – statement).
Assume \Psi is of the regularised Arrhenius type (2.2). Then there exists a critical value R_f^* (of order unity) such that:

· For R_f < R_f^*, the only attracting state is the unburnt equilibrium; all ignitions die out.
· At R_f = R_f^*, a saddle‑node bifurcation of travelling wave solutions occurs.
· For R_f > R_f^*, a stable travelling combustion front with speed c(R_f) > 0 exists, and c increases monotonically with R_f.

Proof outline. Substitute the travelling wave ansatz T(\xi), Y(\xi) with \xi = x-ct into (2.5). The resulting ODE system is analysed in the phase plane. A shooting argument or Conley index calculation shows that a heteroclinic connection between the unburnt and burnt states exists exactly when R_f exceeds a threshold. At threshold the stable and unstable manifolds of the two saddle points coincide, forming a saddle‑node on the invariant circle. The rigorous details, using large‑activation‑energy asymptotics, are given in Chapter 3. ∎

---

2.5 Thermodynamic and Information‑Theoretic Stability Certificates

System (2.5) admits a non‑equilibrium thermodynamic formulation. Define the free‑energy functional

\mathcal{F}[T,Y] = \int_\Omega \Bigl[ c_p T \ln\frac{T}{T_\infty} + \phi(Y) \Bigr] d\mathbf{x},

with \phi(Y) a convex potential. Its time derivative yields the entropy production rate

\Sigma(t) = \int_\Omega \Bigl[ \frac{K(T)|\nabla T|^2}{T^2} + \frac{h(T-T_\infty)^2}{T T_\infty} + \Delta G \, \Psi(T) Y \Bigr] d\mathbf{x} \ge 0,

where \Delta G is the Gibbs free energy of combustion.

Proposition 2.3 (Lyapunov function).
If the wind field \mathbf{w} and ambient temperature are stationary, then \frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F} \le -\Sigma + \mathcal{O}(\epsilon). For a steady travelling wave, \frac{d}{dt}\mathcal{F}=0 and \Sigma attains its steady‑state minimum.

Proof. Differentiate \mathcal{F} using the PDEs and integrate by parts. Divergence terms vanish for insulated boundaries. The \mathcal{O}(\epsilon) term arises from slow moisture and can be absorbed into a perturbed Lyapunov function. ∎

Near criticality the smallest relaxation rate of \mathcal{F} scales as (R_f^* - R_f)^{1/2}, manifesting critical slowing down—a generic early‑warning sign.

Information‑theoretic interpretation.
Let p(\mathbf{x},t) be the probability density of the fire’s spatial state. The mutual information between the early‑time state and the final burnt area, I(t), satisfies

I(t) \sim \frac{1}{2} \log\!\left(\frac{1}{R_f^* - R_f}\right) + \text{const} \quad \text{as } R_f \to R_f^*,
\tag{2.7}

diverging at the tipping point. This result follows from the fluctuation–dissipation theorem and shows that the landscape becomes highly informative about the fire’s fate near criticality.

---

2.6 Geometry of Fire Risk: The Fisher–Rao Manifold

Let \boldsymbol{\theta} \in \mathbb{R}^p be the vector of environmental parameters (fuel moisture, wind speed, slope, etc.). The deterministic model (2.4) generates a map \mathcal{F}: \boldsymbol{\theta} \mapsto \mathbf{x}(T_h;\boldsymbol{\theta}) for a forecast horizon T_h. With additive Gaussian observation noise of variance \sigma^2, the Fisher information metric on the parameter manifold is

g_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \frac{1}{\sigma^2}\sum_k \frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_k}{\partial \theta_i}\frac{\partial \mathcal{F}_k}{\partial \theta_j}.
\tag{2.8}

Regions with large scalar curvature R(\boldsymbol{\theta}) correspond to parameter combinations where small perturbations yield disproportionately large changes in fire behaviour.

Theorem 2.4 (Curvature divergence at criticality).
Suppose the dynamics undergo a fold bifurcation at \boldsymbol{\theta}^*. Then \det g \to 0 and the scalar curvature satisfies

\lim_{\boldsymbol{\theta} \to \boldsymbol{\theta}^*} R(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \infty .

Proof sketch. Near a fold the Jacobian of \mathcal{F} has a singular value that vanishes linearly with the distance to the bifurcation. Consequently the smallest eigenvalue of g vanishes quadratically, and the scalar curvature, which contains the inverse of the determinant, diverges. ∎

This divergence provides a rigorous, model‑based early‑warning signal: a rapid increase of the condition number \kappa(g) or a drop in \det g signals approach to the megafire threshold.

---

2.7 Control‑Theoretic Formulation: Safety and Stabilisation

Suppression (water/retardant drops, firebreaks) enters the PDE as a distributed cooling rate \mu(\mathbf{x},t) \ge 0:

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \cdots - \mu(\mathbf{x},t)\,T .
\tag{2.9}

The safe set \mathcal{C} is defined by

\mathcal{C} = \Bigl\{ (T,Y) : \int_\Omega Y\,d\mathbf{x} \ge Y_\text{safe},\; \sup_{\mathbf{x}} T(\mathbf{x}) \le T_\text{safe} \Bigr\}.

A Control Lyapunov–Barrier Function (CLBF) B: \mathcal{H} \to \mathbb{R} satisfies

1. B \ge 0 on \mathcal{C} and B \to \infty at \partial\mathcal{C};
2. there exists a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha and a control policy \mu such that \dot{B} + \alpha(B) \le 0 on \mathcal{C}.

The existence of a CLBF guarantees forward invariance of \mathcal{C} and asymptotic stability of the unburnt state.

Theorem 2.5 (Controllability threshold).
Consider system (2.5) with control (2.9).

· If R_f < R_f^*, there exists a CLBF and a bounded control policy \mu(\mathbf{x},t) \le \mu_{\max} that keeps the system in \mathcal{C} and stabilises the unburnt equilibrium.
· If R_f > R_f^*, then for any bounded control policy there exist initial conditions in \mathcal{C} from which the trajectory escapes \mathcal{C} in finite time and converges to the megafire attractor.

Proof rationale. For R_f < R_f^* the linearised system is stabilisable, and a high‑gain feedback \mu = k_p T combined with a barrier term can satisfy the CLBF condition. For R_f > R_f^* the existence of a stable travelling wave that cannot be cooled sufficiently by any \mu \le \mu_{\max} implies that a sufficiently large ignition kernel will burn through the safe set. The argument uses parabolic comparison principles. ∎

This theorem formalises the operational reality that, beyond a certain fire intensity, no finite resources can contain the fire—a precise dynamical‑systems definition of a megafire.

---

2.8 From Theory to Prediction

The high‑dimensional state is partially observed via satellite infrared imagery, weather stations, and UAV telemetry. Bayesian filtering (the Ensemble Score Filter) propagates the posterior distribution p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k|\mathbf{y}_{1:k}) using a physics‑informed neural network (PINN) emulator of the forward map. The critical probability

P_{\text{mega}}(t) = \mathrm{Prob}\bigl( R_f(\boldsymbol{\theta}_t) > R_f^* \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:t} \bigr)

and the curvature‑based condition number are computed in real time, providing a seamless bridge from theory to an operational early‑warning system.

Chapter 3
Nonlinear Bifurcation Analysis: The Critical Fire Number and Emergence of Megafire Waves

We now prove that the dynamical system (2.5) undergoes a fold bifurcation of travelling combustion waves, establishing a precise threshold R_f^* for the emergence of a self‑sustaining megafire front. The analysis uses a combination of phase‑plane shooting and large‑activation‑energy asymptotics. The ignition function \Psi(T) is taken to be the smooth regularisation introduced in (2.2); this guarantees differentiability and allows the use of standard invariant manifold theorems while preserving the essential threshold behaviour.

---

3.1 Travelling Wave Reduction

In one space dimension, with a constant wind speed w aligned with the propagation direction, we seek solutions that move with permanent form and constant speed c>0. Adopt the moving coordinate \xi = x - ct and write T = T(\xi), Y = Y(\xi). Substituting into the dimensionless system (2.5) gives

\begin{aligned}
- c T' &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} (\kappa(T)T')' - w T' - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y, \\
- c Y' &= - \beta \Psi(T) Y,
\end{aligned}

with primes denoting differentiation with respect to \xi. Define the effective advection speed v = c - w and rearrange to obtain the system of ordinary differential equations

\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} (\kappa(T)T')' + v T' - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y &= 0, \tag{3.1a}\\
c Y' &= \beta \Psi(T) Y . \tag{3.1b}
\end{aligned}

The boundary conditions describe an unburnt state far ahead of the front and a burnt state far behind:

T(-\infty)=0,\quad Y(-\infty)=1; \qquad T(+\infty)=0,\quad Y(+\infty)=Y_b,
\tag{3.2}

with 0 \le Y_b < 1 the residual fuel mass fraction. Because \Psi(T) is smooth and vanishes for T\le 0 (we set the ambient temperature to zero in dimensionless variables), the equilibria of interest are the unburnt state P_- = (0,0,1) and the burnt line \mathcal{L} = \{(0,0,Y): 0\le Y\le 1\}. A travelling wave corresponds to a heteroclinic orbit connecting P_- to a point on \mathcal{L}.

---

3.2 Phase‑Plane Analysis and Shooting Formulation

Set U = T' and rewrite (3.1) as a first‑order system in \mathbb{R}^3:

\begin{aligned}
T' &= U, \\
U' &= -\mathrm{Pe}\, v\, U + \mathrm{Pe}\,\mathrm{St}\, T - \mathrm{Pe}\,\lambda\,\Psi(T) Y, \tag{3.3}\\
Y' &= \frac{\beta}{c}\,\Psi(T) Y .
\end{aligned}

Lemma 3.1 (Linearisation at the unburnt state).
The Jacobian of (3.3) at P_- has eigenvalues \mu_+>0, \mu_-<0, and 0, where

\mu_{\pm} = \frac{\mathrm{Pe}}{2}\Bigl(-v \pm \sqrt{v^2 + 4\,\mathrm{St}/\mathrm{Pe}}\Bigr).

The zero eigenvalue corresponds to the trivial Y‑direction; the one‑dimensional unstable manifold W^u(P_-) is tangent to the eigenvector associated with \mu_+.

Proof. Evaluate the Jacobian at (0,0,1). Because \Psi(0)=0 (the smooth sigmoid vanishes at the ambient temperature), the T,U equations decouple from Y, giving the stated quadratic for the eigenvalues of the 2\times2 block. The zero eigenvalue reflects the invariance of Y when T=0. ∎

Any trajectory leaving P_- along the unstable manifold can be written, for \xi sufficiently negative where T is below the ignition threshold, as

T(\xi) = C e^{\mu_+ \xi}, \quad U(\xi) = \mu_+ C e^{\mu_+ \xi}, \quad Y(\xi) = 1,

with C>0 an arbitrary constant. Translation invariance allows us to fix the front by demanding that the temperature reaches a prescribed value at \xi=0. A convenient choice is the inflection point of the sigmoid, or simply the point where T=1 (in dimensionless units, a representative ignition temperature). We thus set the initial condition at \xi=0 as

T(0)=1, \quad U(0) = U_0(c,\lambda), \quad Y(0)=1,
\tag{3.4}

where U_0(c,\lambda) is the unique slope on the unstable manifold at T=1. The function U_0 is smooth and, for fixed c, increases monotonically with \lambda.

After the front has passed, the temperature eventually drops below the ignition threshold and \Psi(T)\equiv 0. Let d>0 be the first \xi after the reaction zone where T(d)=1 again, with derivative U_d = T'(d). In the region \xi>d the temperature obeys the linear equation

\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}(\kappa(T)T')' + v T' - \mathrm{St}\, T = 0,

and to satisfy T\to 0 as \xi\to\infty we must select the decaying exponential solution T(\xi) \propto e^{\mu_-(\xi-d)}. Hence the required matching condition at the trailing edge is

U_d = \mu_- .
\tag{3.5}

Thus the existence of a travelling wave reduces to the scalar nonlinear equation

F(c,\lambda) \equiv U_d(c,\lambda) - \mu_-(c) = 0,
\tag{3.6}

where U_d(c,\lambda) is obtained by integrating (3.3) from the initial condition (3.4) forward in \xi until the first return of T to unity. This defines a smooth curve in the (c,\lambda) plane.

---

3.3 Large Activation Energy Asymptotics and the Fold

To resolve the curve defined by (3.6) analytically, we exploit the large activation energy limit. Introduce the small parameter \varepsilon = T_a^{-1} \ll 1 where T_a is the dimensionless activation temperature appearing in the Arrhenius factor \exp(-T_a/T). We construct an asymptotic solution by matched expansions.

Flame‑sheet limit. Outside a thin reaction zone, the chemical reaction is frozen (\Psi=0). The temperature profiles in the preheat zone (\xi<0) and post‑flame zone (\xi>d) are therefore

T_-(\xi) = e^{\mu_+ \xi}, \qquad T_+(\xi) = e^{\mu_- (\xi-d)},

with T'_-(0)=\mu_+, T'_+(d)=\mu_-. The reaction zone is located where T = 1 + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon). Rescaling \zeta = \xi/\varepsilon and expanding T = 1 + \varepsilon \phi + \cdots, the reaction–diffusion balance in the inner layer yields, at leading order, a jump condition relating the gradients on either side:

\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\bigl[\kappa(T)T'\bigr]_{0}^{d} = -\frac{\lambda c}{\beta}(1 - Y_b) + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon).

The fuel consumption equation gives, to leading order, Y_b \sim \exp(-\frac{\beta}{c} \int \Psi \, d\xi). For \varepsilon\to0, complete combustion (Y_b\to0) is approached, and the jump condition becomes

\mu_+ - \mu_- = \frac{\lambda \,\mathrm{Pe}\, c}{\beta}. \tag{3.7}

Substituting the expressions for \mu_{\pm} and using v = c-w (and for simplicity taking zero ambient wind w=0 so that v=c), we obtain

\mathrm{Pe}\sqrt{c^2 + 4\,\mathrm{St}/\mathrm{Pe}} = \frac{\lambda \mathrm{Pe}\, c}{\beta}.

Solving for \lambda gives the flame‑sheet curve

\lambda_0(c) = \beta \sqrt{1 + \frac{4\,\mathrm{St}}{\mathrm{Pe}\, c^2}}.
\tag{3.8}

This function decreases monotonically from \infty as c\to0 to \beta as c\to\infty. No fold exists in the flame‑sheet limit; the travelling wave exists for all \lambda > \beta, and the wave speed is uniquely determined by \lambda.

Exponentially small corrections and the fold.
For finite, small \varepsilon, incomplete combustion introduces an exponentially small correction to the fuel consumption. A refined asymptotic analysis (see [1,2]) shows that the residual fuel fraction is

1 - Y_b \sim \frac{c}{\beta} e^{-f(c)/\varepsilon},

where f(c)>0 is an \mathcal{O}(1) function related to the inner layer structure. Inserting this into the jump condition yields

\mu_+ - \mu_- = \frac{\lambda \,\mathrm{Pe}\, c}{\beta} \Bigl(1 - \frac{\kappa_1 c}{\beta} e^{-f(c)/\varepsilon} + \cdots \Bigr).

Consequently, the effective heat release is reduced at high speeds because the residence time in the reaction zone is too short for complete combustion. Solving for \lambda gives

\lambda(c;\varepsilon) = \lambda_0(c) \Bigl(1 + \frac{\kappa_1 c}{\beta} e^{-f(c)/\varepsilon} \Bigr) + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon).
\tag{3.9}

The key point is that the exponential correction term grows with c, causing \lambda(c) to turn upward at large c. Together with the blow‑up as c\to0, the function \lambda(c;\varepsilon) is convex and possesses a unique minimum at some finite c^*(\varepsilon). The transversality condition \partial\lambda/\partial c = 0 at the minimum is satisfied because the derivative of the correction term becomes negative at small c and positive at large c, ensuring a fold.

Theorem 3.2 (Fold bifurcation of travelling waves).
For every sufficiently small \varepsilon>0 (i.e. sufficiently large activation temperature), there exists a critical heat‑release parameter \lambda^*(\varepsilon) = \min_{c>0} \lambda(c;\varepsilon) and a unique wave speed c^*(\varepsilon) such that:

· For \lambda < \lambda^*, no travelling wave solution exists.
· At \lambda = \lambda^*, there is exactly one travelling wave (the “critical wave”) with speed c^*.
· For \lambda > \lambda^*, there are two travelling wave solutions with speeds c_{\text{slow}}(\lambda) < c^* < c_{\text{fast}}(\lambda).

The corresponding critical value of the Critical Fire Number is

R_f^* = \frac{\lambda^*}{\mathrm{St} + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}} .

Proof. The existence curve \lambda(c;\varepsilon) is smooth, tends to infinity as c\to0 and as c\to\infty, and has a unique minimum. The fold structure follows from the implicit function theorem applied to F(c,\lambda)=0 near the turning point; the transversality \partial F/\partial c =0 is verified using the asymptotic expression. A rigorous geometric proof can be given using the shooting method and the fact that the unstable manifold of P_- and the stable manifold of the burnt point intersect transversely at the fold. ∎

The upper branch (c > c^*) is stable under perturbations; the lower branch is unstable. In the full PDE, a stable travelling front on the upper branch corresponds to a self‑sustaining megafire, while the lower branch is a threshold nucleus that must be exceeded to ignite. Below \lambda^*, no propagating solution exists—all ignitions die out, irrespective of initial size.

---

3.4 Connection to the Full PDE and Two‑Dimensional Effects

The one‑dimensional travelling wave analysis captures the essential mechanism selecting a minimal fire intensity for self‑sustained spread. In two dimensions, the same bifurcation governs the behaviour of a locally straight fire line. The front speed becomes curvature‑dependent, and the dynamics can be described by a level‑set equation with a speed function that exhibits a saddle‑node as a function of the local R_f. Spot‑fire and turbulent effects add stochasticity but do not eliminate the underlying deterministic fold; they may blur the exact threshold but preserve the rapid transition in macroscopic behaviour.

