Chapter 5
Control‑Theoretic Framework: Safety, Stabilisation, and the Controllability Threshold

We now formalise the operational task of fire suppression using the language of control theory. The goal is twofold: to keep the fire state within a prescribed safe set, and if possible, to drive it to extinction. We prove that these dual objectives can be simultaneously achieved if and only if the Critical Fire Number lies below the bifurcation threshold, thereby giving a mathematically rigorous definition of a megafire as a fire that has crossed the boundary of controllability.

---

5.1 Controlled Wildfire PDE and Safe Set

Suppression actions—aerial drops of water or retardant, firebreaks—are modelled as a distributed heat sink. The dimensionless controlled system is

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \nabla\!\cdot\!(\kappa(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\, T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y - \mu(\mathbf{x},t) T, \\[4pt]
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\beta \Psi(T) Y,
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.1}

with \mu(\mathbf{x},t) \ge 0 the local cooling rate due to suppressant. The control input is bounded by the finite capacity of suppression resources: 0 \le \mu(\mathbf{x},t) \le \mu_{\max}, and \mu \equiv 0 outside a compact support. The state space is \mathcal{H} = L^2(\Omega) \times L^2(\Omega). The safe set \mathcal{C} \subset \mathcal{H} is defined by two constraints—sufficient unburnt fuel and temperature below a damage threshold:

\mathcal{C} = \Bigl\{ (T,Y) : \int_\Omega Y(\mathbf{x})\,d\mathbf{x} \ge Y_{\text{safe}},\; \sup_{\mathbf{x}\in\Omega} T(\mathbf{x}) \le T_{\text{safe}} \Bigr\}.
\tag{5.2}

A control policy is any causal function \mu = k(T,Y) (state feedback) or open‑loop strategy \mu(\mathbf{x},t) respecting the bounds.

---

5.2 Control Barrier Functions and Safe Stabilisation

Definition 5.1 (Control Lyapunov–Barrier Function).
A continuously differentiable functional B: \mathcal{H} \to \mathbb{R} is a Control Lyapunov–Barrier Function (CLBF) for (5.1) with respect to \mathcal{C} if:

1. B(T,Y) \ge 0 for all (T,Y) \in \mathcal{C}, and B(T,Y) \to \infty as (T,Y) \to \partial\mathcal{C};
2. there exists a class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha and a control policy \mu satisfying the bounds such that

\dot{B}(T,Y; \mu) + \alpha(B(T,Y)) \le 0 \qquad \forall (T,Y) \in \mathcal{C},
\tag{5.3}

where \dot{B} denotes the Lie derivative of B along the vector field of (5.1).

If a CLBF exists, then the sub‑level sets of B are forward invariant, guaranteeing safety, and B serves as a Lyapunov function that drives the state to the set where B=0—typically the unburnt equilibrium (0,1).

---

5.3 The Controllability Threshold

Theorem 5.1 (Existence of CLBF and the megafire boundary).
Consider the controlled system (5.1) on a bounded domain with homogeneous Neumann boundary conditions. Assume the ignition function \Psi is smooth and of the regularised Arrhenius type (2.2), and let R_f^* be the critical value given by Theorem 3.2.

· If R_f < R_f^*: There exists a CLBF B and a bounded state‑feedback policy \mu(\mathbf{x},t) \le \mu_{\max} that keeps the system in \mathcal{C} and stabilises the unburnt equilibrium.
· If R_f > R_f^*: For any bounded control policy, there exist initial conditions (T_0,Y_0) \in \mathcal{C} such that the trajectory leaves \mathcal{C} in finite time and converges to the megafire attractor. Consequently, no CLBF exists.

Proof.

Part 1 (R_f < R_f^*).
When R_f < R_f^*, the unburnt state (0,1) is asymptotically stable for the uncontrolled system. A quadratic Lyapunov functional V = \frac{1}{2}\int (T^2 + (1-Y)^2)\,d\mathbf{x} satisfies \dot{V} \le -\gamma V for some \gamma>0 under the linearised dynamics. A CLBF is constructed by adding a barrier term that penalises proximity to \partial\mathcal{C}:

B(T,Y) = V(T,Y) - \log\bigl( \mathrm{dist}((T,Y),\partial\mathcal{C}) \bigr).

The barrier term is smooth and goes to infinity at the boundary. With a sufficiently high‑gain linear feedback \mu = k_p T (using the fact that the linearised system is controllable on the slow subspace), the inequality \dot{B} + \alpha(B) \le 0 can be satisfied for a suitably chosen class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha. The boundedness of control is ensured because the required cooling is proportional to the state, and a sufficiently large gain exists while respecting \mu_{\max} for initial conditions in a region of attraction within \mathcal{C}. A rigorous Lyapunov‑based control design for reaction–diffusion systems with input constraints confirms the feasibility (see, e.g., [7]).

Part 2 (R_f > R_f^*).
For R_f > R_f^*, Theorem 3.2 guarantees the existence of a stable travelling wave solution on the infinite line with speed c>0. On a bounded domain, one can embed a sufficiently long segment of this wave into an initial condition (T_0,Y_0) that lies within \mathcal{C}—for instance, a narrow hot strip with abundant fuel on both sides, whose peak temperature is below T_{\text{safe}} and whose total unburnt fuel exceeds Y_{\text{safe}}.

Consider any admissible control \mu(\mathbf{x},t) \le \mu_{\max}. We construct a subsolution by comparing the controlled PDE to a modified travelling wave with a slightly reduced reaction rate. Specifically, define the operator \mathcal{L}[T] = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\nabla\!\cdot\!(\kappa(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda \Psi(T)Y - \mu_{\max} T. Because \mu \le \mu_{\max}, the true cooling is at most \mu_{\max} T; hence any supersolution of the \mu_{\max}‑controlled equation is a supersolution of the actual controlled equation.

Now take the stable travelling wave profile (T_*(\xi), Y_*(\xi)) of the uncontrolled system with speed c_* corresponding to the given \lambda. Since T_* is strictly positive and the wave is robust, we can reduce the effective heat‑release parameter slightly to \lambda' = \lambda - \delta such that the modified wave speed c_\delta is still positive but slightly smaller. By choosing \delta small enough, the additional sink -\mu_{\max} T can be dominated by the reduction \delta \Psi(T)Y, provided the maximum of T_* is bounded and \mu_{\max} is finite (this is a standard perturbative argument in combustion; see [8]). More formally, one compares the \omega‑limit set of the two systems using the parabolic comparison principle: a sufficiently wide initial condition that contains the profile of the modified wave will force the solution to propagate at least at speed c_\delta, regardless of the bounded control. Since the fire is moving, it will eventually consume the available fuel, driving \int Y\,d\mathbf{x} below Y_{\text{safe}} or causing the temperature to exceed T_{\text{safe}} as the front encounters fresh fuel. Thus the trajectory escapes \mathcal{C} in finite time, and no CLBF can exist. ∎

Remark. The proof for R_f > R_f^* relies on the construction of a subsolution that is unaffected by the maximum possible suppression. This avoids the need for a comparison principle for state‑dependent feedback; we merely use the fact that the open‑loop strategy \mu = \mu_{\max} provides the worst‑case cooling. The argument shows that even with the strongest admissible cooling, a sufficiently large fire kernel cannot be stopped.

---

5.4 Implications for Adaptive Suppression

Theorem 5.1 establishes a fundamental operational dichotomy. When R_f < R_f^*, the fire is containable: there exists a feedback strategy that stabilises the system to safety, and real‑time estimation of R_f can guide resource deployment to maximise effectiveness. Once R_f exceeds R_f^*, however, the fire is uncontainable by any finite means—the definition of a megafire. At this point, management must shift from suppression to evacuation and asset protection. The real‑time monitoring of P_{\text{mega}}(t) thus directly informs the highest‑level strategic decision.

