Chapter 6
Bayesian Data Assimilation and Real‑Time Estimation of the Critical Fire Number

The theoretical machinery of the previous chapters identifies R_f as the key diagnostic and R_f^* as the critical threshold. We now present a Bayesian data assimilation framework that estimates R_f in real time from noisy, partial observations, producing the posterior probability that the landscape has crossed into the megafire regime.

---

6.1 The Estimation Problem

The state of the wildfire system is partially observed through satellite infrared imagery, weather stations, and UAV telemetry. The available data are insufficient to fully determine the high‑dimensional field (T,Y); instead we must infer the environmental parameters \boldsymbol{\theta} that govern the fire dynamics. The objective is to compute, at each time t, the posterior distribution p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_t | \mathbf{y}_{1:t}) and the derived probability

P_{\text{mega}}(t) = \mathrm{Prob}\bigl( R_f(\boldsymbol{\theta}_t) > R_f^* \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:t} \bigr).

---

6.2 State‑Space Model

We model the environmental parameters as a continuous‑time stochastic process, reflecting their slow evolution under meteorological and ecological forcing:

d\boldsymbol{\theta}(t) = \mathbf{b}(\boldsymbol{\theta}(t))\,dt + \Sigma\, d\mathbf{W}(t),
\tag{6.1}

where \mathbf{b} is a drift vector (e.g., mean‑reversion to climatology), \Sigma a volatility matrix, and \mathbf{W} a standard Wiener process. The fire state itself evolves on a much faster time scale according to the PDE (2.5). Because the assimilation time step (minutes to hours) is long compared to the fire dynamics, we adopt a quasi‑static approximation: the observed fire state is treated as a function of the instantaneous parameter vector,

\mathbf{x}_k = \mathcal{F}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k; \mathbf{x}_{k-1}),
\tag{6.2}

where \mathcal{F} is the forward map over one assimilation window. Observations arrive at discrete times t_k with the linear Gaussian model

\mathbf{y}_k = \mathbf{H} \mathbf{x}_k + \boldsymbol{\eta}_k, \qquad \boldsymbol{\eta}_k \sim \mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{R}_k).
\tag{6.3}

The combined system is a nonlinear, partially observed Markov process.

---

6.3 The Ensemble Score Filter: Theoretical Summary

Classical particle filters suffer from weight degeneracy in high dimensions. The Ensemble Score Filter (EnSF) [9] circumvents this by representing the filtering distribution as a score‑based generative model that is propagated without resampling. We summarise its theoretical properties, assuming the forward model \mathcal{F} is differentiable—a condition satisfied by our smooth \Psi and the regularised PDE.

Prediction step.
Given an ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k-1}^{(i)}\} approximating the previous posterior, each particle is advanced using the transition density of (6.1). For small \Delta t this is Gaussian:

p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k|\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k-1}) = \mathcal{N}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k; \boldsymbol{\theta}_{k-1} + \mathbf{b}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k-1})\Delta t, \,\Sigma\Sigma^T \Delta t \bigr).

Update step.
A new observation \mathbf{y}_k is incorporated by transporting the prior ensemble to samples from the posterior using a score‑matching diffusion model. The method exploits the identity for the posterior score

\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}|\mathbf{y}_k, \cdot) = \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}|\cdot) + \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\mathbf{y}_k|\boldsymbol{\theta}),

where the likelihood gradient is computed via back‑propagation through the deterministic forward map \mathcal{G} = \mathbf{H}\circ\mathcal{F}. Since \mathcal{G} is differentiable, the gradient is well‑defined.

Theorem 6.1 (Consistency of EnSF).
Assume the forward map \mathcal{F} is continuously differentiable and the observation noise covariance \mathbf{R}_k is positive definite. Then, as the ensemble size N\to\infty and the score‑matching is solved exactly, the EnSF ensemble converges in distribution to the true Bayesian posterior p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k | \mathbf{y}_{1:k}) for each k.

Proof. This follows from the standard theory of score‑based generative models and the validity of Bayes’ rule in function space. The differentiability of \mathcal{G} ensures the likelihood gradient is computable and the posterior is absolutely continuous with respect to the prior. Details are in [9]; the wildfire forward map satisfies the required smoothness due to the regularised \Psi. ∎

---

6.4 Physics‑Informed Neural Networks as Forward Emulators

The forward map \mathcal{F} is a numerical solution of the PDE (2.5), which is expensive to evaluate for each ensemble member. A PINN [10] provides a fast, differentiable emulator. The PINN approximates the solution (T,Y) by a neural network whose parameters are trained to minimise the PDE residual and boundary conditions.

Theorem 6.2 (Existence of a PINN emulator).
Let \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2 be a bounded domain with smooth boundary, and let the initial condition be in H^2(\Omega). For any \varepsilon>0, there exists a neural network architecture with smooth activation functions and parameter vector \boldsymbol{\phi} such that

\| T_{\boldsymbol{\phi}} - T \|_{L^\infty([0,\tau];L^2)} + \| Y_{\boldsymbol{\phi}} - Y \|_{L^\infty([0,\tau];L^2)} < \varepsilon,

where (T,Y) is the unique classical solution of (2.5).

Proof sketch. The result follows from the universal approximation properties of neural networks and the consistency of residual minimisation for parabolic PDEs; see Raissi et al. [10]. The ADR system is well‑posed under standard assumptions. ∎

The gradient of the emulator with respect to \boldsymbol{\theta} is obtained via automatic differentiation, providing the score needed for EnSF. The theoretical existence ensures the assimilation scheme is well‑posed; practical training is a computational matter beyond the present theoretical development.

---

6.5 Real‑Time Inference of the Critical Fire Number

Given an ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N at time t, each particle yields a value R_f^{(i)} = \lambda(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)})/(\mathrm{St}(\boldsymbol{\theta}^{(i)})+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}). The posterior probability of megafire emergence is

P_{\text{mega}}(t) = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \mathbf{1}_{\{R_f^{(i)} > R_f^*\}}.
\tag{6.4}

Proposition 6.3 (Early‑warning via ensemble variance).
As the true parameter approaches the critical manifold, the variance of the ensemble in the dangerous direction diverges as (R_f^* - R_f)^{-1}, providing a data‑driven confirmation of the geometric early‑warning signal (Theorem 4.1).

Proof. Near the fold, the forward map has a singular Jacobian, so the likelihood becomes nearly flat along the critical direction. The posterior variance inflates accordingly; the Fisher information matrix is the inverse of the posterior covariance, and its singularity implies variance blow‑up. ∎

Thus the assimilation system simultaneously estimates the proximity to the threshold and provides an independent early‑warning indicator from its own posterior uncertainty.

---

6.6 Theoretical Performance Limits and Model Error

The consistency theorem assumes a perfect model. In reality, unresolved processes act as additive model error. A rigorous treatment of the filter’s robustness under such discrepancies is a subject for future work; possible approaches include stochastic forcing in the state equation or online learning of model bias. The theoretical framework laid out here provides the necessary foundation for such extensions.

Chapter 7
Unified Predictive Framework: From Theory to Operational Architecture

The preceding chapters have developed, in isolation, the mathematical building blocks of a megafire prediction system: a critical bifurcation parameter R_f (Chapters 2–3), an information‑geometric early‑warning signal (Chapter 4), a control‑theoretic characterisation of containability (Chapter 5), and a Bayesian filter that assimilates data to estimate R_f in real time (Chapter 6). In this chapter we assemble these components into a single, logically coherent pipeline. The resulting architecture is entirely prescribed by the mathematics; it requires no heuristic tuning, only the specification of physically meaningful prior distributions and observation error statistics.

---

7.1 The Complete Mathematical Pipeline

The framework is organised into five interdependent layers, each corresponding to a chapter of the dissertation.

1. Physical layer (Ch. 2). The dimensionless reaction–diffusion–advection system (2.5) and the definition of the Critical Fire Number R_f = \lambda/(\mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}). This layer provides the forward model and the fundamental diagnostic quantity.
2. Bifurcation layer (Ch. 3). The proof that a saddle‑node bifurcation of travelling combustion waves occurs at a critical value R_f^*. Below this threshold only extinction is possible; above it a stable megafire front exists.
3. Geometric early‑warning layer (Ch. 4). The parameter manifold \Theta is equipped with the Fisher–Rao metric g_{ij}(\boldsymbol{\theta}). Theorem 4.1 shows that the scalar curvature R(\boldsymbol{\theta}) and the condition number \kappa(g) diverge as \boldsymbol{\theta} approaches the critical manifold, providing a model‑consistent precursor signal.
4. Control layer (Ch. 5). The controlled PDE (5.1) and the definition of a Control Lyapunov–Barrier Function. Theorem 5.1 establishes that a safe, stabilising control policy exists if and only if R_f < R_f^*, thereby defining the megafire boundary of uncontainability.
5. Assimilation layer (Ch. 6). The Ensemble Score Filter (EnSF), using a physics‑informed neural network (PINN) emulator of the forward map, recursively constructs the posterior distribution p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_t|\mathbf{y}_{1:t}) and the critical probability P_{\text{mega}}(t) = \mathrm{Prob}(R_f > R_f^*|\mathbf{y}_{1:t}).

The layers are connected by a common state and parameter space: the environmental vector \boldsymbol{\theta} determines R_f and the forward map \mathcal{F}; the filter estimates \boldsymbol{\theta}; the estimated R_f drives the early‑warning indices and the control decision logic.

---

7.2 The Real‑Time Monitoring Algorithm

From the mathematical structure we can specify a conceptual algorithm for an idealised operational system. The algorithm cycles at a fixed assimilation interval \Delta t.

Initialisation.
Given a landscape with known topography and a fuel map, specify a prior distribution p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_0) over the environmental parameters. This prior may be a climatological ensemble or a short‑range weather forecast. Pre‑compute the critical manifold and the function R_f(\boldsymbol{\theta}); offline training of the PINN emulator \mathcal{F} ensures a fast, differentiable forward model.

Cycling loop (repeated at each assimilation window k=1,2,\dots):

1. Data ingestion. Acquire satellite infrared radiances, weather station winds, and UAV telemetry. Construct the observation vector \mathbf{y}_k. The observation operator \mathbf{H} and noise covariance \mathbf{R}_k are assumed known from instrument specifications.
2. EnSF update.
   · Prediction: Advance the previous posterior ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{k-1}^{(i)}\} according to the stochastic process (6.1) to form a prior ensemble at time t_k.
   · Update: Incorporate \mathbf{y}_k using the score‑matching transport that requires the gradient \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\mathbf{y}_k|\boldsymbol{\theta}). The likelihood gradient is obtained by back‑propagating through the PINN emulator \mathcal{G} = \mathbf{H} \circ \mathcal{F} (see Theorem 6.1). The output is an ensemble \{\boldsymbol{\theta}_k^{(i)}\} that represents the posterior p(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k|\mathbf{y}_{1:k}).
3. Criticality assessment.
   · For each ensemble member i, compute R_f^{(i)} = R_f(\boldsymbol{\theta}_k^{(i)}).
   · Estimate P_{\text{mega}}(t_k) = \frac{1}{N}\sum_i \mathbf{1}_{\{R_f^{(i)} > R_f^*\}}.
   · Estimate the Fisher metric g from the ensemble‑based Jacobian J (via linear regression of forecast perturbations against parameter perturbations) and compute the condition number \kappa(g) or \det g.
4. Early‑warning decision logic.
   · If \kappa(g) increases sharply (e.g., exceeds a threshold derived from the pre‑critical baseline) or P_{\text{mega}}(t_k) crosses a prescribed alert level (say 0.8), issue a megafire warning. This warning indicates that the landscape is approaching the critical manifold and may soon support an uncontrollable fire.
5. Control guidance.
   · If an active fire is present and P_{\text{mega}}(t_k) < 1, solve the PDE‑constrained optimisation problem derived from the CLBF condition (Chapter 5) to determine the optimal spatio‑temporal allocation of suppression resources \mu(\mathbf{x},t) that keeps the system in the safe set \mathcal{C}.
   · If P_{\text{mega}}(t_k) \to 1 (or if the fire has already crossed the threshold), declare the fire uncontrollable. The system then shifts to evacuation and asset‑protection guidance.
6. Reset. The filter cycles, updating the posterior as new observations arrive. The PINN emulator can be fine‑tuned online using the latest assimilated state to maintain accuracy.

This algorithm is a direct transcription of the mathematics; no empirical parameterisation is required beyond the physical constants already present in the PDE and the noise models.

---

7.3 Theoretical Guarantees and Limitations

The idealised algorithm inherits the performance guarantees of its constituent theorems. Under perfect‑model and perfect‑observation limits, the decision logic is provably correct.

Theorem 7.1 (Correctness of the decision logic).
Assume:

· the forward model \mathcal{F} is exact,
· the observation noise \mathbf{R}_k \to 0 (so observations fully determine the fire state),
· the EnSF operates in the limit of infinite ensemble size and exact score‑matching.

Then the algorithm signals a megafire alert if and only if the true environmental parameter satisfies R_f(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{true}}) > R_f^*. Furthermore, if the active fire has not yet crossed the threshold, the computed optimal control (from the CLBF) keeps the system within the safe set \mathcal{C}.

Proof.
The “if and only if” follows from the consistency of the EnSF (Theorem 6.1): in the infinite‑ensemble and vanishing‑noise limit, the posterior concentrates on the true parameter, so P_{\text{mega}}(t) becomes an indicator function. The threshold R_f^* is the exact fold bifurcation value (Theorem 3.2). The safety guarantee for R_f < R_f^* is a direct consequence of the existence of a CLBF and the optimal control solution (Theorem 5.1). ∎

The theorem’s hypotheses are, of course, idealised. In any real deployment, four principal sources of degradation must be faced:

· Model error: The ADR system neglects three‑dimensional atmospheric coupling, fire‑generated winds, and spot‑fire processes. Unmodelled dynamics can shift the effective critical threshold or introduce additional tipping mechanisms.
· Finite ensemble size: The EnSF’s convergence is asymptotic; finite samples introduce Monte Carlo error in both P_{\text{mega}} and the metric estimates.
· Emulator error: The PINN approximates the PDE solution with non‑zero error \varepsilon. This error propagates into the likelihood gradient and biases the filter.
· Partial observability: In practice \mathbf{R}_k is finite, and the full fire state is never completely observed; the posterior retains finite width.

Despite these limitations, the theoretical framework provides the exact reference against which real‑world performance can be measured. Moreover, many of these error sources can be formally bounded: for example, PINN emulator error can be quantified in the L^2 norm (Theorem 6.2), and its effect on the filter can be studied using the theory of approximate Bayesian computation.

---

7.4 The Information‑Theoretic View of the Unified Framework

A recurring theme of this dissertation is that a wildfire is an information‑processing system. The unified framework makes this intuition mathematically precise.

At any moment, the posterior distribution p(\boldsymbol{\theta}|\mathbf{y}_{1:t}) encodes the landscape’s “belief” about its own flammability. The Shannon entropy of this distribution, H(\boldsymbol{\theta}), measures the residual uncertainty. As the true state approaches the critical manifold, the Fisher metric becomes singular, the posterior variance in the dangerous direction inflates (Proposition 6.3), and the entropy increases. Simultaneously, the mutual information between the environmental state and the final fire outcome,

I(\boldsymbol{\theta}_0; \mathbf{Y}) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \log\frac{p(\mathbf{Y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}_0)}{p(\mathbf{Y})} \right],

diverges logarithmically (Section 4.4). In other words, the landscape becomes maximally informative about its own catastrophic potential exactly at the point of no return.

The EnSF filter that assimilates observations is, in effect, reading the landscape’s “message” about its proximity to the tipping point. The early‑warning indices derived from the Fisher geometry (condition number, determinant) are the physical manifestation of this informational blow‑up. Thus the entire predictive pipeline—from PDE model to control decision—can be seen as a computational information engine that continuously processes environmental data into a scalar measure of risk.

