Appendix A

Nondimensionalisation and Derivation of the Critical Fire Number R_f

We start from the dimensional system (2.1):

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \nabla\!\cdot\!(K(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - h(T-T_\infty) + \Psi(T) Y, \\
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\Psi(T) Y,
\end{aligned}

with K(T)=k+\varepsilon\sigma T^3. Introduce reference scales: length L (fuel bed depth), velocity U (characteristic wind speed), time \tau = L/U, temperature rise \Delta T_c = T_c - T_\infty where T_c is a combustion temperature, and initial fuel mass fraction Y_0. Define

\tilde{T} = \frac{T-T_\infty}{\Delta T_c},\quad \tilde{Y} = \frac{Y}{Y_0},\quad \tilde{\mathbf{x}} = \frac{\mathbf{x}}{L},\quad \tilde{t} = \frac{t}{\tau}.

Let \kappa(\tilde{T}) = K(T)/\alpha_0 where \alpha_0 is a reference thermal diffusivity. The dimensionless groups are

\mathrm{Pe} = \frac{UL}{\alpha_0},\qquad \mathrm{St} = \frac{hL}{U},\qquad \lambda = \frac{A L \Delta H_c Y_0}{c_p \Delta T_c U},\qquad \beta = \frac{A L}{U}.

Substituting and dropping tildes yields

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\nabla\!\cdot\!(\kappa(T)\nabla T) - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda \Psi(T) Y, \\
\frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} &= -\beta \Psi(T) Y.
\end{aligned}
\tag{A.1}

Now consider a global energy balance over a control volume V that contains the fire front. Integrate the T equation over V and apply the divergence theorem. Assuming the control volume moves with the front so that the net advective flux across the boundary represents the energy carried away, we obtain a steady‑state balance between heat release and total dissipation:

\lambda \int_V \Psi(T) Y \, d\mathbf{x} \approx \left( \mathrm{St} + \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} \right) \int_V T \, d\mathbf{x},

where the dissipation includes convective cooling (\mathrm{St}) and effective diffusion (\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}). This motivates the definition

R_f := \frac{\lambda}{\mathrm{St} + \mathrm{Pe}^{-1}}.
\tag{A.2}

The scaling argument is made rigorous by considering the linear stability of the homogeneous unburnt state T\equiv0, Y\equiv1. The linearised equation for T with \Psi'(0)=\gamma>0 (regularised ignition) is

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\nabla^2 T - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda\gamma T.

A Fourier mode e^{\sigma t + i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}} yields the dispersion relation

\sigma = -\frac{|\mathbf{k}|^2}{\mathrm{Pe}} - i\mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{k} - \mathrm{St} + \lambda\gamma.

The real part is \Re(\sigma) = -\frac{|\mathbf{k}|^2}{\mathrm{Pe}} - \mathrm{St} + \lambda\gamma. The most dangerous mode is \mathbf{k}=0, giving instability when \lambda\gamma > \mathrm{St}. In the limit \gamma\to 0 (sharp ignition), the linear criterion is degenerate, and the true threshold emerges from the fully nonlinear bifurcation. Nevertheless, the combination \lambda/(\mathrm{St}+\mathrm{Pe}^{-1}) remains the natural measure of the energy imbalance; setting R_f=1 corresponds to marginal linear stability in the long‑wavelength, diffusion‑dominated limit.

