Appendix B

Linear Stability and the Dispersion Relation

We provide the full linearisation of (A.1) around T\equiv 0, Y\equiv 1. Because \Psi(T) is a smooth sigmoid with \Psi(0)=0 and \Psi'(0)=\gamma>0 (see (2.2)), the linearised equations are

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\nabla^2 T - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda\gamma T,\qquad \frac{\partial Y}{\partial t} = 0.

Let T = \hat{T} e^{\sigma t + i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{x}}. Substituting gives the algebraic equation

\sigma \hat{T} = \left(-\frac{|\mathbf{k}|^2}{\mathrm{Pe}} - i\mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{k} - \mathrm{St} + \lambda\gamma\right)\hat{T}.

Thus \sigma = -\frac{|\mathbf{k}|^2}{\mathrm{Pe}} - \mathrm{St} + \lambda\gamma - i\mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\mathbf{k}. The real part is

\Re(\sigma) = \lambda\gamma - \mathrm{St} - \frac{|\mathbf{k}|^2}{\mathrm{Pe}}.

Setting \Re(\sigma)=0 for \mathbf{k}=0 gives \lambda\gamma = \mathrm{St}. In the limit \mathrm{Pe}\to\infty and with the definition (A.2), this condition becomes R_f = 1, assuming \gamma is normalised appropriately. However, for the precise sigmoid \Psi, \gamma can be made arbitrarily small by increasing the sharpness parameter \delta. In the strict threshold limit \delta\to0, \gamma\to0 and the linear stability becomes neutral; the bifurcation is then purely nonlinear and is captured by the travelling wave analysis. Hence Lemma 2.1 is to be understood as the regularised linear criterion that anticipates the fully nonlinear fold.

