Appendix C

Travelling Wave ODEs and Shooting Formulation

We derive the system (3.3) in full detail. Seek solutions T(\xi), Y(\xi) with \xi = x-ct. Then \partial_t = -c\,\partial_\xi. Equation (A.1) becomes

-c T' = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}(\kappa(T)T')' - w T' - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda\Psi(T)Y,\qquad -c Y' = -\beta\Psi(T)Y,

where primes denote d/d\xi. The second equation gives Y' = \frac{\beta}{c}\Psi(T)Y. Rewrite the first as

\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}(\kappa(T)T')' + (c-w) T' - \mathrm{St}\,T + \lambda\Psi(T)Y = 0.

Introduce U = T' and v = c-w. Then

T' = U,\qquad U' = \frac{1}{\kappa(T)}\Bigl[ -\mathrm{Pe}\,v\,U + \mathrm{Pe}\,\mathrm{St}\,T - \mathrm{Pe}\,\lambda\Psi(T)Y - \kappa'(T)U^2 \Bigr].

For simplicity we neglect the \kappa'(T)U^2 term (it is higher order in the temperature variation) and absorb the constant conductivity into the definition of \mathrm{Pe}; this yields system (3.3). This reduction is standard in combustion theory and does not affect the qualitative bifurcation structure.

Unstable manifold at P_-. The Jacobian at (0,0,1) is

J = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 1 & 0 \\
\mathrm{Pe}\,\mathrm{St} & -\mathrm{Pe}\,v & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0
\end{pmatrix},

with eigenvalues \mu_\pm = \frac{\mathrm{Pe}}{2}(-v \pm \sqrt{v^2 + 4\mathrm{St}/\mathrm{Pe}}) and 0. The eigenvector for \mu_+ is (1, \mu_+, 0)^T. Thus the unstable manifold is locally given by U = \mu_+ T + \mathcal{O}(T^2), Y=1. Integrating this manifold backward from the ignition point gives the initial condition (3.4).

Matching at the trailing edge. For \xi>d, T<1 and \Psi(T)=0. The T equation becomes linear:

\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} T'' + v T' - \mathrm{St}\,T = 0.

The characteristic equation is \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}} r^2 + v r - \mathrm{St} = 0, with roots r_\pm = \mu_\pm (since the same quadratic appears). For T(\infty)=0 we must select the negative root \mu_-. Hence T(\xi) = T(d) e^{\mu_-(\xi-d)} for \xi\ge d. Continuity of derivative requires T'(d^+) = \mu_- T(d). Because T(d)=1, we obtain U_d = \mu_-, which is equation (3.5).

