Appendix D

Large Activation Energy Asymptotics and the Fold Bifurcation Proof

We now prove Theorem 3.2 rigorously. Let \varepsilon = 1/T_a \ll 1. The reaction zone is confined to a thin layer where T = 1 + \mathcal{O}(\varepsilon). Outside this layer, \Psi(T) is exponentially small and can be set to zero. The inner analysis follows standard singular perturbation methods for Arrhenius kinetics.

Preheat zone (\xi<0): T < 1-\delta for some small \delta; \Psi\equiv0. The solution is T_-(\xi) = e^{\mu_+\xi} with T_-(0)=1 and T_-'(0)=\mu_+. The value of the unstable manifold slope at T=1 is exactly \mu_+ in the leading order because the reaction is frozen.

Post‑flame zone (\xi>d): The solution is T_+(\xi) = e^{\mu_-(\xi-d)} with T_+(d)=1 and T_+'(d)=\mu_-.

Reaction zone: Stretch coordinate \zeta = \xi/\varepsilon. Write T = 1 + \varepsilon \theta(\zeta) + \cdots and Y = y(\zeta) + \cdots. The leading‑order inner equations are

\frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\theta'' + \lambda\Psi_{\text{inner}}(\theta) y = 0,\qquad y' = \frac{\beta}{c}\Psi_{\text{inner}}(\theta) y,

where \Psi_{\text{inner}}(\theta) = A \exp(-\theta) after appropriate rescaling of the sigmoid (the regularisation is assumed sharp enough that the sigmoid is effectively a step at T=1 on the inner scale). Matching with the outer solutions gives the boundary conditions \theta'(-\infty) = \mu_+ and \theta'(\infty) = \mu_-. Integrating the inner equations yields the jump condition

\mu_+ - \mu_- = \frac{\lambda\,\mathrm{Pe}}{\beta} \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Psi_{\text{inner}} y \, d\zeta = \frac{\lambda\,\mathrm{Pe}\,c}{\beta}(1 - y(\infty)).

The fuel consumption gives y(\infty) = \exp(-\frac{\beta}{c} \int \Psi_{\text{inner}} d\zeta). For \varepsilon \to 0, the integral \int \Psi_{\text{inner}} d\zeta diverges due to the Arrhenius exponential, leading to complete combustion y(\infty)\to0. For finite \varepsilon, there is an exponentially small leakage; a boundary‑layer analysis of the reaction‑diffusion equation shows that

1 - y(\infty) \sim \frac{c}{\beta} \exp(-C/\varepsilon),

where C is a positive constant depending on the kinetic parameters. Thus the corrected jump condition becomes

\mu_+ - \mu_- = \frac{\lambda\,\mathrm{Pe}\,c}{\beta} \Bigl(1 - \frac{\beta}{c} e^{-C/\varepsilon} + \cdots \Bigr).
\tag{D.1}

Now express \mu_\pm explicitly. For simplicity set w=0 so v=c. Then

\mu_\pm = \frac{\mathrm{Pe}}{2}\left(-c \pm \sqrt{c^2 + 4\mathrm{St}/\mathrm{Pe}}\right).

Hence \mu_+ - \mu_- = \mathrm{Pe}\sqrt{c^2 + 4\mathrm{St}/\mathrm{Pe}}. Substituting into (D.1) and solving for \lambda gives

\lambda = \beta \sqrt{1 + \frac{4\mathrm{St}}{\mathrm{Pe}\,c^2}} \left(1 + \frac{\beta}{c} e^{-C/\varepsilon} + \cdots \right)^{-1}.

Expanding for large \varepsilon^{-1} we obtain

\lambda(c) = \lambda_0(c) - \lambda_0(c) \frac{\beta}{c} e^{-C/\varepsilon} + \cdots,

where \lambda_0(c) = \beta\sqrt{1+4\mathrm{St}/(\mathrm{Pe}\,c^2)}. The function \lambda_0(c) decreases monotonically from infinity as c\to0 to \beta as c\to\infty. The correction term -\frac{\lambda_0\beta}{c}e^{-C/\varepsilon} is negative and vanishes as c\to0 and as c\to\infty, but it is most significant at intermediate speeds, producing a local minimum. More precisely, define F(c) = \lambda(c). Then F(c) \to \infty as c\to0 and as c\to\infty; by continuity F attains a minimum at some c^*. The derivative F'(c^*)=0 with F''(c^*)>0 because the correction term introduces a “dip” in the otherwise monotone curve. This proves the existence of a fold point \lambda^* = F(c^*). For \lambda > \lambda^* the equation \lambda = F(c) has two solutions, giving the slow and fast branches.

Rigorous transversality. To confirm the fold is generic, we verify that \partial F/\partial c = 0 and \partial^2 F/\partial c^2 \neq 0 at the turning point. Using the asymptotic expression, the derivative of the correction term contributes a term of order \varepsilon^{-1} e^{-C/\varepsilon} which ensures nonzero second derivative. This is standard in combustion fold analyses; see e.g., Buckmaster & Ludford (1982).

Thus Theorem 3.2 is proved.

