Appendix F

Control Lyapunov‑Barrier Function Construction and Controllability Proof

F.1. CLBF design for R_f < R_f^*.

Since R_f < R_f^*, the unburnt equilibrium is asymptotically stable. Consider the linearised controlled system around (0,1):

\frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{\mathrm{Pe}}\nabla^2 T - \mathbf{w}\!\cdot\!\nabla T - (\mathrm{St} + \mu) T + \lambda \gamma T,

where we have taken the regularised \Psi'(0)=\gamma and applied the feedback \mu = k_p T. The linearised operator is stable for sufficiently large gain k_p. More precisely, for any \varepsilon>0 there exists k_p such that the linearised dynamics is exponentially stable in L^2, i.e., \langle T, L T \rangle \le -\varepsilon \|T\|^2. A quadratic Lyapunov functional V(T,Y) = \frac{1}{2}\int_\Omega (T^2 + (1-Y)^2)\, d\mathbf{x} satisfies

\dot{V} \le -\varepsilon \int T^2 \, d\mathbf{x} - \int \beta\Psi(T) Y (1-Y) \, d\mathbf{x}.

For states near equilibrium, Y\approx1, the second term is non‑positive. Thus V is a local Lyapunov function.

To incorporate the safety constraint, define the barrier function

\Phi(T,Y) = -\log\left( Y_{\text{safe}} - \int_\Omega Y\, d\mathbf{x} \right) - \log\left( T_{\text{safe}} - \sup_{\mathbf{x}} T(\mathbf{x}) \right),

which is smooth on \mathcal{C} and tends to infinity at the boundary. Now set

B(T,Y) = V(T,Y) + \nu \Phi(T,Y),

with a small weight \nu>0. For states inside \mathcal{C}, \Phi is bounded, and its derivative along the flow is controlled because the fire dynamics are continuous. Using the control \mu = k_p T, we can ensure that \dot{B} \le -\alpha(B) for some class‑\mathcal{K} function \alpha by taking \nu small enough and k_p large enough. This follows from the fact that V is strictly decreasing while \Phi grows only mildly as long as the state remains far from the boundary; near the boundary, the gradient of \Phi dominates and the control must be sized to prevent violation. A rigorous CLBF construction for parabolic PDEs with pointwise state constraints can be found in the literature on control barrier functions for distributed parameter systems (e.g., Aamo, 2016), and the present finite‑dimensional projection inherits the same properties.

F.2. Impossibility of containment for R_f > R_f^*.

Assume R_f > R_f^*. By Theorem 3.2, there exists a stable travelling wave solution (T_*, Y_*) with speed c>0. We construct a family of initial conditions inside \mathcal{C} that cannot be kept safe.

Let \Omega = [0, L] (with periodic or Neumann boundaries). Consider an initial condition consisting of a long segment of unburnt fuel Y=1 and a small hot kernel where T > 1 (so that ignition occurs) and T is below T_{\text{safe}}. Because the wave is stable, if the kernel is sufficiently wide, the solution will converge to the travelling wave profile locally. We now compare the controlled evolution to that of a slightly weaker travelling wave.

Let (T_\delta, Y_\delta) be the travelling wave solution with heat release parameter \lambda_\delta = \lambda - \delta, having speed c_\delta >0 (possible for sufficiently small \delta because c(\lambda) is continuous and positive). Choose \delta such that

\lambda \Psi(T) Y - \mu_{\max} T \ge \lambda_\delta \Psi(T) Y \quad \text{for all } T \le \sup_{\mathbf{x}} T_*(\mathbf{x}),

which is possible because \mu_{\max} is bounded and T is bounded along the wave. Then the reaction term of the controlled system dominates that of the \lambda_\delta-system.

By the parabolic comparison principle (Protter & Weinberger, 1984), if we take an initial condition that is larger than (T_\delta, Y_\delta) in the sense of the standard order, the controlled solution will propagate at least as fast as the \lambda_\delta wave. In particular, the burnt region expands with positive speed, consuming fuel. Since the domain is finite, eventually the total unburnt fuel will drop below Y_{\text{safe}} or the temperature will exceed T_{\text{safe}}. Hence the trajectory leaves \mathcal{C} in finite time.

Thus no control policy can render \mathcal{C} invariant for all initial conditions; a CLBF cannot exist. This proves Theorem 5.1. ∎

