Appendix G

Ensemble Score Filter Consistency and PINN Emulator Error Bounds

G.1. EnSF convergence.
We provide a brief justification of Theorem 6.1. The EnSF uses a diffusion‑based generative model to transport samples from prior to posterior without resampling. Consider the true Bayesian update:

p(\boldsymbol{\theta}|\mathbf{y}) \propto p(\boldsymbol{\theta}) p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}).

The score of the posterior is \nabla \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}|\mathbf{y}) = \nabla \log p(\boldsymbol{\theta}) + \nabla \log p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}). The prior score can be estimated from the ensemble using score matching (Hyvärinen, 2005). The likelihood score is computed by differentiating the log‑likelihood. Because p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}) is Gaussian with mean \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta}), we have

\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \log p(\mathbf{y}|\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \frac{1}{\sigma^2} J_{\mathcal{G}}^T (\mathbf{y} - \mathcal{G}(\boldsymbol{\theta})),

where J_{\mathcal{G}} is the Jacobian of \mathcal{G} = \mathbf{H}\circ\mathcal{F}. If \mathcal{F} is differentiable (which holds for our regularised PDE), then J_{\mathcal{G}} exists and is continuous. The EnSF uses a reverse diffusion stochastic differential equation to sample from the posterior, and in the infinite‑time, infinite‑ensemble limit it converges to the exact posterior under standard assumptions (see Bao et al., 2023). Therefore, as N\to\infty and with exact score matching, the ensemble converges in distribution to the true posterior.

G.2. PINN emulator error bound (Theorem 6.2).
We outline the proof using the theory of Raissi et al. (2019). Let u=(T,Y). The PDE can be written as \mathcal{N}[u]=0 with appropriate initial/boundary conditions. A PINN u_{\boldsymbol{\phi}} with parameters \boldsymbol{\phi} minimises the loss functional

\mathcal{L}(\boldsymbol{\phi}) = \| \mathcal{N}[u_{\boldsymbol{\phi}}] \|^2_{L^2(\Omega\times[0,\tau])} + \text{initial/boundary penalties}.

Because the ADR system is parabolic and well‑posed, the solution operator is continuous. By the universal approximation theorem for neural networks, for any \varepsilon>0 there exists a network architecture and parameters \boldsymbol{\phi} such that the PDE residual and initial/boundary errors are smaller than a constant times \varepsilon. Using energy estimates for the linearised equation, one can show that the L^2 error between the true solution and the PINN is bounded by the residual. Thus \|u_{\boldsymbol{\phi}} - u\| \le C\varepsilon. This proves the existence of an \varepsilon-accurate emulator. The gradient accuracy follows from the smoothness of the network.

